      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{62df7cd83e007e467295c4e1|full_name} The answer here is more complicated than you think. (and there's very little I like talking about more than complicated, obscure Star Trek trivialities)\n\nA \"stardate\" is a fictional time system used by Star Trek. However, Star Trek has had many iterations, as it's a franchise that has been around since the 1960s. It's expressed as a number of digits with a decimal seperator.\n\nEach series has different definitions and sometimes those definitions are murky and shift within the same series. [Memory Alpha's](https://memory-alpha.fandom.com/wiki/Stardate) article is a good primer.\n\nOur calculation was derived from a combination of the Stardate wiki page, as well as a GitHub repo which re-implemented a now-defunct PHP script on a Star Trek fan site, which _itself_ referenced [this page](https://trekguide.com/Stardates.htm). \n\nHere's a very rough, untested psuedocode python implementation of our method\n\nhttps://pastebin.com/tsV21nGC",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
